; EXPERIMENTAL EXAMPLE: a modal-logic fragment
;
; This file demonstrates that Kurt theories can introduce modal prefix operators and axiom
; schemata. It covers box (□, necessity), diamond (◇, possibility), their duality, distribution
; over and/or, the K distribution axiom, and the T axiom in its dual form `p implies ◇p`.
;
; This is not a complete proof system for normal modal logic K or T: Kurt cannot currently
; express the necessitation rule "from a theorem p infer □p". In particular, do not describe
; `load modal` as implementing system K or T. There are also no 4/5 axioms or
; accessibility-relation semantics. See proofs/modal-logic/ for executable examples.
;
; With classical propositional logic and the duality below, `p implies ◇p` is equivalent to
; the standard T axiom `□p implies p`. It is not the seriality axiom D (`□p implies ◇p`).
load prop
prefix b 20, d 20          ; box and diamond
bool b, d
alias □ b
alias ◇ d

; p and q are declared variables once instead of carrying a `%` on every occurrence. Every
; stored formula renames its free variables to fresh internal schema names, so these rules stay
; general after `load` and cannot capture an importer's p or q.
var p, q
use ◇p ≡ ¬□¬p   "diamond-def"
use □p ≡ ¬◇¬p   "box-def"
use ◇p ∨ ◇q ≡ ◇(p ∨ q)   "diamond-distrib-or"
use □p ∧ □q ≡ □(p ∧ q)   "box-distrib-and"

; K distribution axiom
use □(p ⇒ q) ⇒ (□p ⇒ □q)    "K"

; T axiom, in its dual form
use p ⇒ ◇p   "T"
